{"type": "result", "session_id": "20261018_143235_40ac38", "exit_code": 0, "text": "Across all matched pairs, the words that most distinguish the Britannica vocabulary from the Wikipedia vocabulary (ranked by z-score from the corpus comparison) are:\n\n| Word | z-score | Britannica count | Wikipedia count |\n|------|---------|------------------|-----------------|\n| british | 5.547 | 65 | 52 |\n| inches | 4.573 | 25 | 4 |\n| avoirdupois | 4.422 | 20 | 0 |\n| rays | 4.258 | 37 | 28 |\n| let | 4.201 | 19 | 1 |\n| abdomen | 4.13 | 21 | 4 |\n| parallel | 4.095 | 23 | 7 |\n| taste | 4.077 | 17 | 0 |\n| native | 4.051 | 29 | 17 |\n| oil | 3.947 | 21 | 6 |\n| proportion | 3.947 | 21 | 6 |\n| carbonate | 3.849 | 17 | 2 |\n\nSource: corpus-level vocabulary comparison of all matched entries (challenge_corpus): 80 Britannica documents vs. 75 Wikipedia documents, frequency cosine 0.5188. The counts cover only entries with an accepted match. The strongest Britannica-distinguishing term is \"british\" (z = 5.547), followed by measurement/units words (\"inches,\" \"avoirdupois\") and terms like \"rays,\" \"let,\" \"abdomen,\" and \"taste\" — several of which appear in Britannica but rarely or never in the matched Wikipedia articles.\n\nNote: this is the Britannica-distinctive side only. The reverse side (distinctive_wikipedia) is headed by terms like \"century,\" \"steam,\" \"edward,\" \"erigeron,\" and \"erfurt,\" which are far more frequent in the matched Wikipedia articles. I did not verify individual entry scans, so these are the corpus-level figures as returned.", "tokens": {"input": 8449, "output": 1645, "total": 10094, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write": 0}, "duration_ms": 7001, "timestamp": 1792326762623}
